WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Gaia and Hubble have traced the Milky Way's first major collision to a dwarf galaxy swallowed nearly 12 billion years ago. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 165. The two observatories combined proper motion data from Gaia with deep imaging from Hubble to identify stars that once belonged to a dwarf galaxy accreted early in the Milky Way's history. The remnant stars now occupy the Galactic Halo and show distinct chemical patterns and orbital energy.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:48.160
This event is the earliest large scale merger yet confirmed and occurred when the Milky Way was still assembling its disk. The combined data set also reveals how the incoming galaxy stars were dynamically heated and mixed over time.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:01:15.840
Follow-up spectroscopy of additional Halo stars is planned to refine the mass and orbit of the original dwarf. The Milky Way's stellar Halo stretches across roughly 150,000 light years, yet most of its stars arrived in a handful of ancient mergers rather than forming in place. One dwarf galaxy swallowed 12 billion years ago, contributed a population now spread over a volume comparable to the distance between Earth and the Galactic Center.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:34.520
Yet those stars occupy only a few percent of the Halo's total mass. When the dwarf fell in, its orbital energy was converted into random motions that puffed the stars into the extended Halo we see today, much like dropping a compact ice cube into a swirling pot of soup and watching the fragments disperse.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:57.320
The chemical signatures, lower iron and higher magnesium relative to Milky Way disc stars, still tag these interlopers even after billions of years of mixing. We can map their orbits with Gaia's micro arc second precision, but the exact mass and internal structure of that original dwarf remain uncertain because later dynamical heating has erased the cleanest orbital clues.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:07.880
The open question is whether similar early mergers delivered the seeds of the galaxy's oldest globular clusters or whether those clusters formed separately before being captured.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:22.620
From reconstructing the galaxy's distant past, we turned to hardware preparing for its own historic firsts in the near future. NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope is scheduled to lift off within the next 14 days on a Falcon Heavy.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:41.360
The observatory carries a wide field infrared instrument designed to conduct a large-scale exoplanet census and dark energy survey. A new podcast episode outlines how Roman will detect thousands of planets via micro-lensing and directly image young Jupiters around sun-like stars.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:56.420
Integration and testing at Kennedy Space Center are complete. Launch preparations continue at Cape Canaveral. While Roman looks outward to planets and cosmology, another European weather satellite is finishing its own final checks on the ground.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:13.420
The second media sat third-generation imager satellite finished propellant loading and moved into final integrated testing at Europe's spaceport. Once in geostationary orbit, the satellite will deliver higher resolution imagery and lightning mapping for European weather services.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:23.720
Lift off remains scheduled for 27 August aboard an Arian 6. Ground teams are now verifying thermal and electrical interfaces before encapsulation.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:37.660
The mission continues the transition to the MTG constellation that will operate through the 2030s. From satellites heading into steady operational orbits, we shift to a starship that spent nearly a month drifting at sea.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:45.720
Recovery teams guided the starship vehicle to a location off Christmas Island following nearly a month adrift in the Indian Ocean.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:53.560
Engineers are now aborting to inspect thermal protection tiles and structural integrity before the vehicle is towed back to starbase.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:04.420
The extended sea trial provides data on long duration exposure to salt water and wave of loading. Analysis will inform future recovery procedures for reusable upper stages.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:19.620
No immediate timeline for return to Texas has been released. Remember, we covered starship development yesterday and today's sea trial moves that work forward by delivering direct measurements of how the upper stage holds up after weeks in a marine environment.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.800
After long duration hardware testing at sea, we look at new infrared views of a familiar planetary nebula.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:40.000
The James Webb Space Telescope observed NGC 2392 in near and mid infrared wavelengths, revealing fine dust structures and ionized gas in the nebula's face-like appearance.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:48.480
Earlier Hubble images showed the outer envelope. Webb's data penetrate deeper to expose previously unseen knots and filaments.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:55.340
The observations help refine models of how planetary nebulae disperse heavy elements into the interstellar medium.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:03.560
The data were released as part of an ongoing program studying evolved stars. Additional filters are scheduled for later this year.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.020
From nebula chemistry in our own galaxy, we moved to a surprising mineral find on the surface of Mars.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:20.040
Perseverance identified corundum, the mineral that forms rubies and sapphires, within a rock whose texture suggests shock metamorphism from ancient impacts.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:29.200
Most previously sampled Martian minerals traced to volcanic or aqueous processes. This find points to a separate high pressure history.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:35.620
The detection was made with the rover's pixel and Sherlock instruments during a traverse across the crater rim.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:44.980
Scientists will compare the composition with laboratory-shocked terrestrial samples. Further drilling is planned to assess how widespread such minerals may be.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:50.820
From surface mineralogy on Mars, we turned to new high-resolution data on one of its moons.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:06:02.820
During its March 2025 Mars encounter, the Hara spacecraft obtained high-resolution images and spectra of demos that are now being used to model possible impact events on the moon.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:09.740
The data shows surface features consistent with ejecta from larger impacts on nearby fobos or Mars itself.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:17.280
Mission teams are integrating these observations with numerical simulations to constrain the timing and energy of past collisions.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.600
The results will feed into planning for future fobos' demos sample return concepts.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:32.300
Additional analysis is expected in the coming months. From satellite encounters at Mars, we shift to a comet that split in two over the past six months.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:42.440
Ground-based telescopes track the comet that divided into two components over six months, recording changes in brightness, separation velocity, and dust production.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:49.420
The observations allow reconstruction of the fracture event and subsequent outgassing that widen the pair's orbit.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:55.340
Fragmentation models suggest the split occurred near Parahelion when thermal stresses peaked.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:04.520
Continued monitoring will determine whether either piece survives the next orbit. The study provides a rare real-time view of cometary destruction mechanics.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:10.540
From active comets in the present, we look back to a historic discovery made during a solar eclipse.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:24.180
On August 18, 1868, astronomers Pierre Jansen and Norman Lockyer independently recorded a bright yellow line in the sun's spectrum during totality that did not match any known element.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:30.040
Lockyer proposed the line belong to a new element he named Helium after the Greek sun god.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.680
The identification remained the only noble gas first found in space rather than on Earth.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:44.540
Laboratory confirmation came decades later. The anniversary highlights how eclipse spectroscopy opened the study of stellar composition.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:56.940
From historical spectroscopy, we move to modern planetary defense modeling. Researchers modeled the use of a nuclear device to alter the trajectory of an asteroid detected only months before impact.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:06.640
The simulation shows that a stand-off detonation could impart sufficient impulse while minimizing fragmentation risk if the body is larger than a few hundred meters.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:12.880
The work examines variables including yield, stand-off distance, and asteroid rotation state.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:24.040
Current planetary defense road maps still prioritize kinetic impactors for longer warning times. Further studies will assess detection thresholds needed to enable such options.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:37.620
From planetary defense concepts, we turn to commercial imaging contracts for upcoming missions. Intuitive machines will provide spacecraft and operation support for the Eagle VSWIR hyperspectral imaging mission.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:49.000
The contract covers integration of the visible to shortwave infrared sensor and mission unique avionics, launch is planned within the next several years on a rideshare opportunity.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:57.620
The data will support agriculture, mineral mapping, and climate applications. Work begins immediately at the company's Houston facility.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:15.060
From new commercial missions, we recognize long-running government research programs. Engineers at Glenn Research Center conducted icing, emissions, and propulsion tests aboard research aircraft for more than 50 years, validating designs that later flew on commercial and military fleets.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:22.160
Data collected in actual cloud and atmospheric conditions complemented wind tunnel results and reduced development risk.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:29.180
The program logged thousands of flight hours across icing research, alternative fuels, and boundary layer studies.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:36.760
Many findings remain embedded in current certification standards. Archival reports are being digitized for public access.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:42.840
From established research contributions, we turn to congressional discussion of space-derived benefits.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:52.740
Senator and former astronaut Mark Kelly stated that technologies developed for spaceflight have directly contributed to terrestrial medical treatments that save lives today.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:58.640
He referenced closed-loop life support systems and remote diagnostics now used in hospitals.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:08.880
The remarks came during congressional discussion of the agency's budget request. Kelly emphasized that continued investment sustains both exploration and applied benefits.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:18.860
No specific funding figures were released in the statement. From public advocacy for NASA funding, we close with analysis of classified spending trends.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:29.380
The analysis shows that recent increases in space for spending have concentrated in classified programs, missile warning architectures, and command and control systems.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:39.540
Unclassified line items grew more modestly over the same period. The report examines obligation rates and contract types across the past five fiscal years.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:47.200
Authors note that visibility into classified accounts remains limited. The findings will inform upcoming authorization debates.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:59.200
New data continue to refine our picture of how galaxies assemble and how hardware performs in orbit. Keep an eye on additional Roman space telescope integration updates ahead of its Falcon Heavy liftoff.

